{"id":"bang-patrol-6","title":"BANG rescues Ensign Donald E. Corzine","short":"Patrol 6 · Lifeguard, April 1945","eyebrow":"Sakishima Gunto · BANG's sixth war patrol, lifeguard for the Okinawa campaign","lede":"Hit by flak over Ishigaki airfield on the afternoon of 21 April 1945, a CHENANGO Hellcat pilot spent fourteen hours in his raft before BANG, sent from her lifeguard station off Formosa, took him aboard. BANG's report, his squadron's action report and the Miyako garrison's telegram of the same day, on one GMT clock.","places":[{"name":"Ishigaki","lon":124.17,"lat":24.47,"small":true},{"name":"Iriomote","lon":123.82,"lat":24.42,"small":true},{"name":"Miyako","lon":125.33,"lat":24.62,"small":true},{"name":"Yonaguni","lon":123.0,"lat":24.38,"small":true},{"name":"Formosa","lon":121.4,"lat":24.0,"inset":[120.6,23.0]}],"notes":[{"head":"Reading the map.","text":"BANG's track runs straight between her reported positions; her search southeast of Ishigaki on the night of the 21st and the pickup itself have no position in her report, so the line doesn't show them. The dashed ring is where the squadron's report puts Corzine's water landing, \"about twenty miles East of the Island\": an estimate, not a fix. Her 2200 radar-log position (125°14′E as typed) contradicts her own narrative and is drawn dashed."},{"head":"Clocks.","text":"BANG, CHENANGO's air group and the Miyako garrison all kept zone I (−9), Tokyo time, on 21–22 April 1945, so their local times can be read side by side."},{"head":"The Japanese side.","text":"No Japanese record of Corzine's loss has been found. The Miyako-jima Guard Unit's action summary for the 21st (sent 2243) claims one plane shot down and two damaged over Miyako, with no time or place; it is shown for context and is not his. Ishigaki's own report for the day is still missing."},{"head":"Sources.","text":"BANG's sixth war patrol report, NARA microfilm M1752 (74802934), narrative pages 11–13 and the Section U radar-intercept log. Air Group 25's aircraft action reports, Report No. 50 (NARA 140031168). The Navy's Nansei Shoto telegram file via JACAR (C19010021200, National Institute for Defense Studies). Coastline: Natural Earth."}],"boats":[{"id":"bang","name":"BANG","hull":"SS-385","co":"Cdr A. R. Gallaher","patrol_no":6}],"others":[{"id":"chenango","name":"CHENANGO","type":"fighter"}],"actions":[{"id":"corzine","label":"21–22 April 1945","convoy":"","target_label":"Downed aviator","start":"1945-04-21T03:00Z","end":"1945-04-22T04:00Z","route":"Ishigaki airfield strike, USS CHENANGO (Air Group 25).","ships_note":"","outcome":"Shot down by medium flak over Ishigaki airfield and ditched about 20 miles east of the island; the other three Hellcats saw him in his raft. BANG, ordered from her station northeast of Formosa at 1415, reached the reported position at 2330 and, guided in by carrier planes the next morning, took him aboard at 0737 after fourteen hours in the water. \"Replacement clothing was no problem except for his size thirteen shoes.\"","ships":[{"name":"Ens. Donald E. Corzine","kind":"F6F-5 Hellcat, USS CHENANGO; rescued by BANG","type":"fighter","sunk":false}],"escorts":[],"sil_note":"Silhouettes show the kind of ship (fleet submarine, destroyer, escort, auxiliary gunboat, merchant ship, tanker, fighter aircraft), not each one's own outline, and are not to scale.","box":[122.433,23.717,125.583,24.867],"tracks":{"bang":[{"boat":"bang","utc":"1945-04-20T03:00Z","lat":24.0833,"lon":122.1,"kind":"noon","ref":"Noon","on_track":true,"local":"1945-04-20 1200 (I)","raw":"24-05N 122-06E","note":"","fix":"","page":"rg367-74802934/p11","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0157.jpg"},{"boat":"bang","utc":"1945-04-21T03:00Z","lat":24.5667,"lon":122.7833,"kind":"noon","ref":"Noon","on_track":true,"local":"1945-04-21 1200 (I)","raw":"24-34N 122-47E","note":"1415 ordered to Sakishima Gunto to search for downed aviators","fix":"","page":"rg367-74802934/p12","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0158.jpg"},{"boat":"bang","utc":"1945-04-22T03:00Z","lat":24.05,"lon":124.2667,"kind":"noon","ref":"Noon","on_track":true,"local":"1945-04-22 1200 (I)","raw":"24-03N 124-16E","note":"0737 rescued Ens. Donald E. Corzine (USS CHENANGO Hellcat pilot) after 14 h in the water (account on report page 12)","fix":"","page":"rg367-74802934/p13","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0159.jpg"},{"boat":"bang","utc":"1945-04-22T03:00Z","lat":24.0167,"lon":124.2,"kind":"radar","ref":"Radar Apr 22","on_track":true,"local":"1945-04-22 1200 (I)","raw":"24-01N 124-12E","note":"Radar intercept 104 MC (Section U); the narrative's noon position is 24-03N 124-16E","fix":"","page":"rg367-74802934/p36","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0182.jpg"},{"boat":"bang","utc":"1945-04-23T03:00Z","lat":24.6667,"lon":122.6167,"kind":"noon","ref":"Noon","on_track":true,"local":"1945-04-23 1200 (I)","raw":"24-40N 122-37E","note":"","fix":"","page":"rg367-74802934/p14","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0160.jpg"}]},"fixes":[{"boat":"bang","utc":"1945-04-21T03:00Z","lat":24.5667,"lon":122.7833,"kind":"noon","ref":"Noon","on_track":true,"local":"1945-04-21 1200 (I)","raw":"24-34N 122-47E","note":"1415 ordered to Sakishima Gunto to search for downed aviators","fix":"","page":"rg367-74802934/p12","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0158.jpg"},{"boat":"bang","utc":"1945-04-21T13:00Z","lat":24.1,"lon":125.2333,"kind":"radar","ref":"Radar Apr 21","on_track":false,"local":"1945-04-21 2200 (I)","raw":"24-06N 125-14E","note":"Radar intercepts 156 MC and a possible land-based beacon (Section U, two entries at this time and position)","fix":"Typed 125-14E (both entries) puts BANG 40 nm south of Miyako-jima, but the narrative (p12) has her southwest of Iriomote-jima at 2150 and arriving southeast of Ishigaki-jima at 2330; 123-14E would fit the first but not the second. Unresolved, so kept off the track","page":"rg367-74802934/p35","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0181.jpg"},{"boat":"bang","utc":"1945-04-22T03:00Z","lat":24.05,"lon":124.2667,"kind":"noon","ref":"Noon","on_track":true,"local":"1945-04-22 1200 (I)","raw":"24-03N 124-16E","note":"0737 rescued Ens. Donald E. Corzine (USS CHENANGO Hellcat pilot) after 14 h in the water (account on report page 12)","fix":"","page":"rg367-74802934/p13","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0159.jpg"},{"boat":"bang","utc":"1945-04-22T03:00Z","lat":24.0167,"lon":124.2,"kind":"radar","ref":"Radar Apr 22","on_track":true,"local":"1945-04-22 1200 (I)","raw":"24-01N 124-12E","note":"Radar intercept 104 MC (Section U); the narrative's noon position is 24-03N 124-16E","fix":"","page":"rg367-74802934/p36","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0182.jpg"}],"events":[{"utc":"1945-04-21T03:00Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"Posit Lat. 24-34N, Long. 122-47E.","local":"1945-04-21 1200 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p12","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0158.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T04:48Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"Surfaced. Weather clear and seas moderating.","local":"1945-04-21 1348 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p12","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0158.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T05:15Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"Received orders to leave present lifeguard station and proceed to point in vicinity of Sakishima Gunto to search for downed aviators. Proceeding at four engine speed.","local":"1945-04-21 1415 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p12","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0158.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T06:30Z","side":"chenango","type":"record","title":"Four F6F-5s launched","text":"Four F6F-5s were launched from the U.S.S. CHENANGO at 1530 \"I\", 21 April 1945, as a combination strike and Target Combat Air Patrol over Ishigaki Shima. Principal attention was given to the Ishigaki airfield.","local":"1945-04-21 1530 (I)","doubt":"","page":"NARA 140031168, ACA Report No. 50, image p248","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/id/140031168?objectPage=248","ship_type":"fighter"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T08:37Z","side":"chenango","type":"ditched","title":"Corzine ditches; hit by AA over Ishigaki airfield","text":"On one of the bombing runs on Ishigaki airfield, Ensign Donald Elmer CORZINE ... received several hits, probably from medium A.A. ... He was able to maintain control over his plane and flew it about twenty-miles East of the Island before being forced to make a water landing. The other three Hellcats circled him ... and ascertained that he was in his life raft.","local":"1945-04-21 1737 (I)","doubt":"The report gives no time or position for the landing. The time is BANG's \"in the water fourteen hours\" counted back from 0737; the position is 20 miles east of Ishigaki airfield. Both estimated.","page":"NARA 140031168, ACA Report No. 50, image p248","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/id/140031168?objectPage=248","ship_type":"fighter","lat":24.33,"lon":124.5,"estimated":"about 20 miles east of Ishigaki airfield (squadron report); estimated"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T10:00Z","side":"chenango","type":"record","title":"The other three return","text":"Because of the late hour, it was impossible for the Dumbo plane to attempt a rescue that evening. Rescue was effected the following morning by submarine. The other three pilots returned safely to base at 1900 \"I\".","local":"1945-04-21 1900 (I)","doubt":"","page":"NARA 140031168, ACA Report No. 50, image p248","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/id/140031168?objectPage=248","ship_type":"fighter"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T12:50Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"Southwest of Iriomote Jima encountered strong 156 mc radar interference similar to that encountered by BATFISH on Jap submarine (see section U). Swung ship to determine bearing, but found it to be emanating from the island.","local":"1945-04-21 2150 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p12","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0158.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T13:00Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"(Section U intercept log, ship position 24°06'N 125°14'E as typed: 156 mc, PRF 500, pulse width 10, antenna rotation irregular; second entry same time and position:) This had the looks of a 103MC carrier modulated with 100% modulation at 350 CPS. It made constant sweeps at 1 R.P.M. Maybe a Jap radar beacon - land based. (As typed, 24-06N 125-14E is about 40 nm south of Miyako Jima, not southwest of Iriomote Jima as the narrative says at 2150. It does fit the run from the 21 Apr noon position at four-engine speed (about 135 nm east in 7.6 h); unresolved.)","local":"1945-04-21 2200 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p35","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0181.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T13:43Z","side":"japan","type":"status","title":"","text":"Action summary for the 21st. On the 21st, carrier planes (fighters and bombers combined), 83 sorties in all, in formations of 8 to 10-odd, strafed and bombed the Army and Navy airfields in four waves. From 0455 to 0645, B-24s, 2 to 4 at a time, patrolled overhead, strafing and bombing, for about two hours or more; at 0510 one B-24 bombed the airfield; from 1100, two of them circled overhead for an hour and a half, mainly reconnoitring. Results: one shot down, two damaged. No damage to us.","original":"二十一日戦闘概報 二十一日来襲艦上機戦爆聯合延八三機八乃至十数機ノ編隊ニテ四次ニ亘リ陸海軍飛行場銃爆撃自〇四五五至〇六四五B二四二機乃至四機ニテ上空哨戒銃爆撃在空時間概ネ二時間以上〇五一〇B二四一機飛行場爆撃同二機一一〇〇ヨリ一時間半ニ亘リ上空ヲ廻リ主トシテ偵察 戦果撃墜一 撃破二 被害ナシ","local":"1945-04-21 2243 (I, Tokyo)","page":"jacar:C19010021200 p42","scan":"https://www.jacar.archives.go.jp/das/image/C19010021200","doubt":"","ship_type":null},{"utc":"1945-04-21T14:30Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"Arrived at reported position of downed aviators and commenced search southeast of Ishigaki Jima.","local":"1945-04-21 2330 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p12","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0158.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T20:00Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"Began feeling distant explosions and seeing occasional flashes, presumed to be carrier planes striking Ishigaki Jima and Miyako Jima.","local":"1945-04-22 0500 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p12","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0158.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T21:30Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"Began making contact with carrier planes. Requested information on any downed aviators and after a brief flurry of communications learned that we were within 20 miles of a downed Hellcat pilot in rubber boat. Planes furnished cover and guided us in.","local":"1945-04-22 0630 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p12","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0158.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T22:37Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"Took aboard Ensign Donald E. Corzine, file #382983, U.S.S. CHENANGO Hellcat pilot. He had been in the water fourteen hours but was in excellent condition and very glad to be aboard. Replacement clothing was no problem except for his size thirteen shoes. ... this rescue did more for the morale of the crew than anything else could have done with the exception of sinking ships. Every officer and man is proud to have been of some help to those who are now the ball carriers. (No position or cause of loss given. Fourteen hours before 0737 I puts him in the water about 1737 I on 21 Apr.)","local":"1945-04-22 0737 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p12","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0158.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T22:40Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"Proceeding with search for downed aviators previously reported in our orders.","local":"1945-04-22 0740 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p13","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0159.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-21T23:00Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"Released fighter cover. Thereafter during the day numerous planes were contacted, mostly flying high enroute to or from their carriers.","local":"1945-04-22 0800 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p13","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0159.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-22T03:00Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"Posit Lat. 24-03N, Long. 124-16E.","local":"1945-04-22 1200 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p13","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0159.jpg"},{"utc":"1945-04-22T03:00Z","side":"bang","type":"log","title":"Log","text":"(Section U intercept log, ship position 24°01'N 124°12'E: 104 mc, PRF 350, pulse width very wide, antenna rotation 1.) The full scale sweep is as seen in the sketch. Made regular sweeps.","local":"1945-04-22 1200 (I)","page":"rg367-74802934/p36","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/microfilm-publications/M1752-US_SubWarPatrolReports_1941-194/M1752_00051-00075/images/0182.jpg"}],"sinkings":[],"estimates":[{"utc":"1945-04-21T08:37Z","side":"chenango","type":"ditched","title":"Corzine ditches; hit by AA over Ishigaki airfield","text":"On one of the bombing runs on Ishigaki airfield, Ensign Donald Elmer CORZINE ... received several hits, probably from medium A.A. ... He was able to maintain control over his plane and flew it about twenty-miles East of the Island before being forced to make a water landing. The other three Hellcats circled him ... and ascertained that he was in his life raft.","local":"1945-04-21 1737 (I)","doubt":"The report gives no time or position for the landing. The time is BANG's \"in the water fourteen hours\" counted back from 0737; the position is 20 miles east of Ishigaki airfield. Both estimated.","page":"NARA 140031168, ACA Report No. 50, image p248","scan":"https://catalog.archives.gov/id/140031168?objectPage=248","ship_type":"fighter","lat":24.33,"lon":124.5,"estimated":"about 20 miles east of Ishigaki airfield (squadron report); estimated"}],"land":[[[120.851,27.942],[120.855,27.935],[120.862,27.918],[120.862,27.903],[120.869,27.882],[120.851,27.859],[120.826,27.849],[120.808,27.835],[120.797,27.805],[120.769,27.773],[120.74,27.734],[120.722,27.715],[120.704,27.713],[120.686,27.727],[120.653,27.754],[120.635,27.764],[120.635,27.746],[120.646,27.734],[120.671,27.712],[120.675,27.705],[120.679,27.686],[120.671,27.678],[120.668,27.668],[120.671,27.659],[120.675,27.659],[120.